Indicates unimplemented code by panicking with a message of "not implemented".</p>
Th im <elided chars="1789"/></truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>unreachable</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line"><p>
Indicates unreachable code.</p>
This is useful any time that the compiler can't example: <elided chars="1145"/></truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>vec</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line"><p>
Creates a <inline-code>Vec</inline-code> containing the arguments.</p>
<inline-code>vec!</inline-code> allows <inline-code>Vec</inline-code>s to be defined with the same syntax as There are two forms of this macro: <elided chars="926"/></truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>write</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line"><p>
Writes formatted data into a buffer.</p>
This macro accepts a 'writer', a format formatted according to the specified format The writer may be any value with a <inline-code>write_fmt</inline-code> method; generally this comes from an implementation of either the <inline-code>fmt::Write</inline-code> or the <inline-code>io::Write</inline-code> trait. The macro returns whatever the <inline-code>write_fmt</inline-code> method returns; commonly a <inline-code>fmt::Result</inline-code>, or an <inline-code>io::Result</inline-code>. <elided chars="2077"/></truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>writeln</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line"><p>
Writes formatted data into a buffer, with a newline appended.</p>
On all platforms,<inline-code>\n</inline-code>/<inline-code>U+000A</inline-code>) alone (no additional<inline-code>\r</inline-code>/<inline-code>U+000D</inline-code>). <elided chars="471"/></truncated>
</item>
</list>
</section><section><section-title>Primitives</section-title><list>
  <item><p>
<type-name>array</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line">A fixed-size array, denoted <inline-code>[T; N]</inline-code>, for the element type, <inline-code>T</inline-code>, and the non-negative compile-time constant size, <inline-code>N</inline-code>. <elided chars="5525"/></truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>bool</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line"><p>
The boolean type.</p>
The <inline-code>bool</inline-code> represents a value, which could only be either <inline-code>true</inline-code> or <inline-code>false</inline-code>. If you cast a <inline-code>bool</inline-code> into an integer, <inline-code>true</inline-code> will be 1 and <inline-code>false</inline-code> will be 0. <elided chars="1023"/></truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>char</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line"><p>
A character type.</p>
The <inline-code>char</inline-code> type represents a single character. More specifically, since 'character' isn't a well-defined concept in Unicode, <inline-code>char</inline-code> is a 'Unicode scalar value'. <elided chars="3613"/></truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>f128</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line">A 128-bit floating-point type (specifically, the "binary128" type defined in <elided chars="883"/></truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>f16</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line">A 16-bit floating-point type (specifically, the "binary16" type defined in IEEE <elided chars="705"/></truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>f32</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line">A 32-bit floating-point type (specifically, the "binary32" type defined in IEEE <elided chars="9717"/></truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>f64</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line">A 64-bit floating-point type (specifically, the "binary64" type defined in IEEE <elided chars="241"/></truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>fn</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line"><p>
Function pointers, like <inline-code>fn(usize) -> bool</inline-code>.</p>
<emphasis>See also the traits </emphasis><inline-code>Fn</inline-code><emphasis>, </emphasis><inline-code>FnMut</inline-code><emphasis>, and </emphasis><inline-code>FnOnce</inline-code><emphasis>.</emphasis> <elided chars="11049"/></truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>i128</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line"><p>
The 128-bit signed integer type.</p>
<title>ABI compatibility</title>
Rust's <inline-code>i128</inline-code> is expected to be<inline-code>__int128</inline-code> on platforms where the type is available, which includes <inline-code>__int128</inline-code> are updated to introduce it,<inline-code>i128</inline-code> ABI on relevant targets will to match. <elided chars="580"/></truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>i16</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line"><p>
The 16-bit signed integer type.</p>
</truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>i32</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line"><p>
The 32-bit signed integer type.</p>
</truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>i64</type-name> </p>
