The 8-bit signed integer type.</p>
</truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>isize</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line"><p>
The pointer-sized signed integer type.</p>
The size of this primitive is how many location in memory. For example, on a 32 and on a 64 bit target, this is 8 bytes. <elided chars="138"/></truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>never</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line"><p>
The <inline-code>!</inline-code> type, also called "never".</p>
<inline-code>!</inline-code> represents the type of computations which the <inline-code>exit</inline-code> function <inline-code>fn exit(code: i32) -> !</inline-code> exits the process without ever returning, and so returns <inline-code>!</inline-code>. <elided chars="6684"/></truncated>
</item>
  <item><p>
<type-name>pointer</type-name> </p>
<truncated level="single-line"><p>
Raw, unsafe pointers, <inline-code>*const T</inline-code>, and <inline-code>*mut T</inline-code>.</p>
<p>
<emphasis>See also the </emphasis><inline-code>std::ptr</inline-code><emphasis> module</emphasis><emphasis>.</emphasis></p>
Working can be<inline-code>null</inline-code>. pointer,valid for tuple of in-bound. <elided chars="2971"/></truncated>
